        <body>
            <div xml:id="d698">

                <pb facs="0732" n="722" xml:id="pg722"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">834.00 Revolutions/23: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Paraguay</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Howard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Asunción</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-08-15T10:00:00-04:00">August 15, 1937—10
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 12:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>19. Department’s 11, Aug. 14, 2 p.m. In my opinion present disturbance here
                    primarily is due to internal politics. However, the Chaco question has been made
                    the football thereof and the overturn may have a bearing upon the Paraguayan
                    attitude.</p>
                <p>Although I do not believe that the delay of territorial discussions was the
                    motive of the present movement it seems likely that whatever government is
                    formed may attempt to urge the change of government as a pretext for delaying
                    such discussions. It is believed that the present movement was promoted by the
                    old Liberal leaders and their attitude may possibly be reflected in the new
                    government.</p>
                <p>Until a new government is formed and the personnel thereof known it is impossible
                    to conjecture.</p>
                <p>Repeat to Buenos Aires.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Howard</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
